A||N||11||atarian||86' Gt that hasn't ran since the late 80's||||1||
Z||000000||atarian||08-10-2001||04:58 AM||||I am going north to pick up a 86' Gt that has not ran since the late 80's. When it was parked in a garage the engine still ran. Does anyone know what would be the best procedure to bring this car back to life short of rebuilding the engine. I do know the fluids will need to be changed. the gas tank and fuel lines would need to be cleaned (but how do you clean petrol that has turned to jelly) any suggestions apreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hank is Here||08-10-2001||07:11 AM||||Well I would say.  <BR>1) Remove spark plugs, drop kerosene in the cylinders ( just an oz. or so in each.) <BR>2) Let sit for a few days <BR>3) repeat the first step with a light oil, and let sit for a few more days<BR>4) Then turn the motor over by hand, do not do this with the starter!  <BR>5)Then change the oil, if you can try finding a preluber which forces oil into the gallies by pressure from the bottom up.  <P>I am not sure the gas would be bad actually, we are talking about good old late 80s gas, not the modern stuff.  I have fired up a old truck with gas from 82 with no gumming in the carb or tank.  But the gas could very easily be gummed too.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-10-2001||10:55 AM||||follow the above instructions.. but instead of looking for a preluber.. just pull the fuel pump relay fuse, and the coil wire/dist cap and use the starter.. the engines oil pump will lube it up for ya..  (have a battery charger near) just turn it over till you see that oil pressure is building... your lubrication issues are done... move on to spark/fuel etc.||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||08-10-2001||11:12 AM||||Atarian, you might look up Cheever's email addy here on the forum. He is from Missouri City, not far from you. Lone Star Fiero's is the owners club in your area I believe.<BR>Don ||||reg||1
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||08-10-2001||11:48 AM||||If theres not a lot of gas in the tank maybe 5 gals.of some good premium gas thrown in might help you out.I would also look in the distributer  and make sure that there is no corsion and rust in there.I have a feeling you may be surprised at how easy this car will start.after it is running you might have some oil leaks from dried out gaskets.And should change radiator hoses.I cant wait to here how this turns out for you.Please keep us informed.||||reg||1
Z||000005||atarian||08-11-2001||01:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by starfighter007@msn.com:<BR><B>If theres not a lot of gas in the tank maybe 5 gals.of some good premium gas thrown in might help you out.I would also look in the distributer  and make sure that there is no corsion and rust in there.I have a feeling you may be surprised at how easy this car will start.after it is running you might have some oil leaks from dried out gaskets.And should change radiator hoses.I cant wait to here how this turns out for you.Please keep us informed.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the input! The car has 68k miles on the odometer and should drive fine on the way back, as far as gaskets, hoses and belts. I will have them replaced before i leave. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||atarian||08-11-2001||01:22 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>Atarian, you might look up Cheever's email addy here on the forum. He is from Missouri City, not far from you. Lone Star Fiero's is the owners club in your area I believe.<BR>Don </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Good Idea thanks!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||08-11-2001||01:28 AM||||The gas might still be useable, but I'd bet the farm that the fuel pump and/or the sending unit is wasted.<P>Talk to FieroDave about this!  I bought a couple of cars from him (that big California find) and all of the rubber stuff inside the gas tank was melted.<P>I would drop the tank to see what it looks like and replac stuff as needed (fuel pump, sending unit, etc.).<P>Good luck.....it sounds like a fun project.  ||||reg||6
Z||000008||Mach10||08-11-2001||02:59 AM||||I bought a long-term stored Fiero. Most of the rubber stuff disintegrated pretty soon after purchase. That and the oil in the pan was sludge. Before your first REAL oil change (after you run for a while) I'd recommend dumping a can or flushing agent in the engine, and run it until the Oil Pressure starts to drop. Then do a full change. You'd be surprised at how filthy and sloppy that oil will come out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR>Expect your cooling system to start springing leaks, from dried, worn out hoses, and cooling system corrosion. The glycol anti-freeze will separate from the water after a while, and the almost pure water will have at least started to eat through the fittings. I lost a water pump to this. When I got it apart, the impeller looked rusty, and the seals were GONE. Other than that, expect a few minor issues at first, one or two larger ones, but the rest should be smooth sailing. Oh, and your 0-2 sensor's probably died of old age. Mine did.||||reg||6
Z||000009||dgroves||08-11-2001||12:50 PM||||I am working on an '86 GT also. The previous owner had destroyed the origional 2.8 V-6 and attempted to install a junkyard engine with ~80K miles. No telling how long the donor car had been sitting before the motor was scavenged. <P>I entered the project thinking there would be very little to do in order to make a driveable toy. NOT!!!!<P>To get the engine to run I had to replace the injectors, the EGR tube, the fuel pump relay, new tanks for the radiator,the oil pressure sender, the temp sender and several of the vacum lines. The motor will run and drive now but the fuel pump dosen't activate like it should (The oil pressure sender turns it on, not the ECM driver.) and there are still some tuning issues. (Idle speed, and it sets a 32 code)<P>Now the car sat long enough for the brakes to rust. So far I have replaced the vacum booster and the master cylinder and the car still has very little brakes. I guess rotors and calipers are next.<P>My $300 bargain is now over $800 not counting time and gas looking for parts in salvage yards. <P>But you know what? The little bit of driving time has me hooked. This is one fun puppy!!!<P>Good luck on your project. Maybe you will have better luck than I did. Nevertheless the reward should be worth the cost when you are done.||||reg||1
Z||000010||atarian||10-03-2001||02:35 AM||||I updated on new topic...<BR>86Gt 2.8 injector question and a update <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||RossT||10-03-2001||04:05 AM||||My current 88gt was put in storage back in 1988 with only 900 miles on it. There it sat in a warehouse until May of 2000. The car had only been started and moved a few times in 12 years. The only problem I had was a gummed up fuel sending unit that would not work all the times.  I had to remove the tank and take it apart to clean it up.  Other than that, I have had no leaks and no further problems. I did change all the fluid.<BR>The clutch and brake fluid was very nasty, but the original antifreez looked good.  Also the gas left in the tank was not to bad either.  I suggest you change all the fluids first before driving it.  You may want to drop the tank just to make sure there not alot of crap floating around. Also change the fuel filter.<P>I aslo ran a can of cleaner thru the gas and oil to clean things out without any ill effects (used seafoam).<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
